Recent analyses of lepton flavor universality (LFU) violating decays have suggested that new physics beyond the Standard Model may exist. To explore this possibility, we've analyzed the impact of new physics on the decay $\Lambda_b 	o p 	au  ar{
u}$ within the context of the $U_1$ leptoquark model, which accounts for such violations. By linking new physics contributions to the $b 	o c 	au  ar{
u}$ sector with $\Lambda_b 	o p 	au  ar{
u}$ decay, we found that the former can influence the latter in specific ways related to the polarization of the tau lepton and hadronic form factors. In order to gauge the magnitude of these effects, we performed a thorough reevaluation of the latter, considering new physics constraints arising from analyses of the decays $B 	o D 	au  ar{
u}$ and $B_s 	o 	au  ar{
u}  ar{s}$. Our analysis revealed that in the $U_1$ leptoquark model, a combination of maximal $SU(2)_L$ symmetry breaking and large scalar form factor values could potentially increase the branching ratio of the decay $\Lambda_b 	o p 	au  ar{
u}$ to around $10^{-2}$, which could be verified through direct detection at the Large Hadron Electron Collider (LHeC).
